  "text": "I want to start by thanking the audience for inviting me to speak to a group of researchers. My aim is to say something that might matter to people who do experiments, even if the connection is a bit unusual. Modern experimental fields—physics, chemistry, biology, and the like—have split into so many sub‑areas that the old broad categories are almost meaningless. What sets the study of divine matters apart from all other kinds of thinking is that it belongs to a realm beyond the natural world. By this I mean the study of revealed truth, not the philosophical approach that treats the divine as a metaphysical concept. This discipline is not just applying ordinary methods to special material; its very way of seeking and understanding truth shares the special character of the subject it examines."
